# Generated documentation and presets (rebuilt during make generate)
# Source of truth files and what they generate:
#
# Keyboard Shortcuts (from config/shortcuts.yaml):
#   -> web/src/store/shortcutsData.generated.ts
#   -> generated/docs-source/settings/keyboard-shortcuts.generated.md
#
# Workflow/schema/model/tool reference docs:
#   -> generated/docs-source/reference/*.md
#   -> docs/reference/*.mdx
#
# Preset reference docs:
#   -> generated/docs-source/workflows/presets-reference.generated.md
#   -> docs/workflows/presets-reference.generated.mdx
#
# Changelog:
#   -> docs/data/releases/*.yaml
#   -> docs/changelog.mdx
#
# Workflow Builder Skill (from generated docs + runtime reference data):
#   -> internal/skills/catalog/builtin/workflow-builder/SKILL.md

# ── Generated code is COMMITTED ──
#
# The docgen output under generated/, the Go reference files, the YAML
# bindings, the sqlc output under internal/db/postgres/generated/, the
# driver mocks and the generated TypeScript are all checked in
# deliberately.
#
# They used to be ignored on the reasoning that `make generate` re-creates
# them, so committing them was redundant. What that costs is worse than the
# duplication: a fresh clone or `git worktree add` does not compile until
# someone runs codegen, which means CI, a new contributor, and every
# throwaway worktree all start from a broken tree. It also makes generator
# upgrades invisible — a proto or sqlc change produces no diff to review,
# so nobody sees what changed until it breaks at runtime.
#
# The rule now is that a checkout builds as-cloned. Regenerating must be a
# no-op: if `make generate` produces a diff, commit it in the same change
# that caused it.
#
# NOTE: the old rule here was a bare, unanchored `generated/`, which also
# swept up internal/db/postgres/generated/ as a side effect — the sqlc
# output the DB layer needs to compile at all.

# Temp build artifacts (docgen binaries - use `go run` instead of building)
/schema
/refcheck
/scenarios
/celref
/shortcuts
/assembler
/nodes
/models
# ...and the same binaries when built in place next to their main.go. The
# rules above are root-anchored, so they never matched tools/docgen/<x>/<x> —
# which is how two of them ended up tracked.
tools/docgen/*/assembler
tools/docgen/*/celref
tools/docgen/*/changelog
tools/docgen/*/cli
tools/docgen/*/models
tools/docgen/*/nodes
tools/docgen/*/refcheck
tools/docgen/*/scenarios
tools/docgen/*/schema
tools/docgen/*/shortcuts
tools/docgen/*/tools
tools/docgen/*/types
# Cloud-split service binaries (built by go build without -o dist/)
/api-server
/temporal-worker
/tools-daemon
/daemon-gateway
/autotune
